const faq=[['What is the avalanche method?','The avalanche method applies extra payments to the debt with the highest interest rate while maintaining minimums on other debts.'],['What is the snowball method?','The snowball method applies extra payments to the smallest outstanding balance first, potentially creating earlier wins.'],['How is interest saved calculated?','The calculator compares the chosen strategy with a baseline that pays listed minimums and rolls freed-up minimum payments into remaining debts. It does not assume that simply sharing debt creates automatic savings.'],['Does marriage combine credit card or student loan debts?','Marriage does not automatically merge your accounts or make every debt jointly owned. Responsibility depends on the loan, location and other circumstances. Seek local legal guidance for your situation.']];
---
<BaseLayout title="Couples Debt Payoff Calculator | Avalanche vs Snowball | DollarAngle" description="Free couples debt payoff calculator. Combine balances, compare avalanche vs snowball methods, project your debt-free date and interest without signing up.">
<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)}/>
<div class="container debt-page"><nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/tools">Money Tools</a> › Couples Debt Payoff</nav>
<header class="intro"><p class="kicker">FREE COUPLES DEBT CALCULATOR</p><h1>Debt-Free Marriage Timeline: <span>Find Your Payoff Date</span></h1><p>Getting married does not have to mean hiding debt from each other. Compare two repayment strategies, combine your payment plan and explore what extra monthly payments could accomplish.</p></header>
<CouplesDebtPayoff />
<article class="guide"><h2>How to build a debt payoff plan as a couple</h2><p>List each debt’s balance, interest rate (APR) and contractual minimum monthly payment. Enter the additional amount you can comfortably pay each month, then compare paying the highest-interest debts first (avalanche) with paying the smallest balances first (snowball). Both methods preserve listed minimum payments and redirect remaining budget toward prioritized debts.</p>
<h2>Debt avalanche versus debt snowball</h2><p><strong>Debt avalanche</strong> often reduces interest expense by tackling expensive borrowing first. <strong>Debt snowball</strong> can make it easier to stay motivated by clearing smaller balances sooner. The best choice is the one you can consistently follow.</p>
<h2>What does the projected payoff date assume?</h2><p>The estimate applies monthly interest at APR divided by 12, assumes a steady fixed monthly payment budget, does not add new borrowing or fees, and rolls unused payments toward other balances. Actual lender schedules, interest compounding, introductory rates and debt terms may differ.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>{faq.map(([question,answer])=><details><summary>{question}</summary><p>{answer}</p></details>)}
<h2>Further reading and tools</h2><p><a href="/articles/marrying-someone-with-100k-debt">Would you marry someone with $100,000 in debt?</a> <a href="/tools/mine-yours-ours-budget-calculator">Plan shared bills and personal spending</a></p>
<p class="disclaimer">DollarAngle provides educational estimates, not individualized financial or legal advice. Some loans, especially student loans and mortgages, have special terms. Check lender statements and repayment conditions before acting.</p></article></div>
</BaseLayout>
